rong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.89 to +1.52) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.56\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.20 to +0.93). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in none; 3 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Bakersfield's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.01 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 6.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1998 (13.32 in); the driest, 1959 (1.87 in). Bakersfield almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.1 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}